{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b2ff-60d1-7b92-bcfc-8a4c766633f3"},"sequence":67,"recorded_at":1780531400000129,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71"},"call_id":"call_01a0b2ff88bd703f8e8d54b533de9ef1","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-kkh1an/TASK-260830-kkh1an_producer.md`.\n1|Own TASK-260830-kkh1an (implement-terminal-instance-state-machine), the SECOND leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe (ax-pane-and-terminal-instance-binding, EPIC M1). Predecessor TASK-260830-1geqhj (the `ax pane` enforcement wrapper decision core) is accepted and checkpointed on the Story branch; the final leaf TASK-260830-2056mm (terminal binding events and recovery) follows you and owns the Story-close items. Implement, as production code, the Terminal Instance lifecycle contract of pinned Section 4.C over the LANDED internal/terminalbackend package — that package already owns `InstanceState`/`ParseInstanceState` (the eight-state closed enum absent|creating|parked|active|quiescing|stopped|stale_fenced|unavailable), `Operation`/`ParseOperation`, `SideEffect`/`ParseSideEffect`, `Transition`/`CheckTransition`, `CheckErrorAllowed`, `IdempotencyKey`, `Receipt`/`Ledger`/`ImportLedger`, `StatusResult`/`CheckStatusResult`, `AttachAuthorization`/`CheckAttachRequest`/`CheckAttachResult` and the Manifest/Probe/descriptor admission. Do NOT fork a second state machine or a second enum: extend that package (or add a sibling package that drives it) and state in the results exactly which existing type or gate each addition extends.\n2|Scope of this leaf — the parts of 4.C the landed code does NOT yet model:\n3|(1) AXAuthorization as a closed object (exactly lease_id:UUIDv4, lease_epoch:uint53[1..9007199254740991], holder_host_id:UUIDv7 and the remaining members exactly as the pinned section lists them, no extra and no missing member, unknown member refused), parsed through the landed canonicaljson closed-shape discipline, with the epoch bound witnessed at 1, 9007199254740991 and both adjacent out-of-range values; today `Transition.Authorization` is only a string, so every operation's authorization requirement must become a checked object.\n4|(2) The closed RetryDisposition enum — exactly replay_same, status_first, new_authorization, required_operator_action — carried by every result, plus the request/result identity contract: every mutating request carries operation, Session, Terminal Instance and idempotency IDs, the exact backend ID/version/generation, a deadline and an expiring AX authorization with the winning lease ID/epoch; every result repeats those identities and adds before/after state, side effects, evidence IDs and exactly one retry disposition.\n5|(3) The state-entry rules, each one driven and each one mutated: `creating` is entered only by `create`, only after its durable idempotency receipt and before its first listed side effect; an error BEFORE a side effect restores the source state; an error AFTER an effect whose result cannot be proven moves the local observation to `unavailable` with `status_first` and MUST NOT claim `absent`; no operation other than `quiesce-input` enters `quiescing`; no backend operation enters `stale_fenced` — only AX fencing observation does (drive that from the landed internal/fencing projection, do not invent a second fencing model); authorization AND generation are rechecked immediately before each side effect (not once at entry); a deadline cancels waiting but never a committed effect.\n6|(4) Generation-safe evidence: `backend_generation` is the canonical string[1..256] bound in every containing contract — reject lengths 0 and 257 and accept 256 in the Terminal Instance Binding, the Provider Protocol 3 descriptor, every operation body and the CLI Result 4 summary projection, and make a stale generation refuse with the landed `terminal_backend_stale_generation` class rather than a fresh code.\n7|(5) The operation table rows for status, quiesce-input, wait-safe-boundary and request-stop exactly as pinned: their source→target states, their listed side effects from the closed TerminalBackendSideEffect enum, their exact idempotency key material (UTF-8 `terminal_instance_id + \"/quiesce/\" + quiescence_generation`, `… + \"/boundary/\" + quiescence_generation + \"/\" + provider_proof_kind`, `… + \"/stop/\" + safe_boundary_evidence_id`), their evidence kinds (input_quiescence, safe_boundary_observation, provider_process_observation, graceful_stop) and their exact admitted refusal sets — a refusal code outside the row's set must be rejected by `CheckErrorAllowed`, and `status` timeout/read failure is `unknown`, never `absent`.\n8|Tests: every state transition and every refusal row driven through the production entry with positives and negatives; the closed enums pinned as closed (an added, removed or re-spelled member must redden a test, and a member census must count copies, not names); receipt/idempotency replay proving an identical retry replays and a changed operation in-window is `idempotency_mismatch`; crash evidence at the durable receipt seam (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) proving `creating` is never entered before the receipt is durable and that a lost result recovers through `status` rather than `absent`; a narrowing mutant per gate (drop or weaken each recheck, each bound, each disposition mapping) executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control; and a conformance matrix over every 4.C clause. No `ax` CLI, no tmux/ConPTY process control and no provider process — those are modeled callers; state that bound explicitly.\n9|COMMON CONTRACT (non-first, non-final leaf of a Story whose predecessor leaves are already checkpointed). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named above; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260917-110onn) may land on trunk during your run and is byte-identical in these sections — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) and above all the packages your own predecessor leaf just added on this Story branch — never fork a second model of anything already modeled; extend or adopt, and say in the results which existing type or gate you extended. Traceability: do NOT edit internal/traceability (ownership registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) in this leaf — the Story's FINAL leaf carries the registry bindings and the re-pin; document your clause coverage in a package-local TRACEABILITY.md and in the conformance matrix instead. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) or an explicit purity bound; conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; README package section with test commands and NO capability/CLI claim (no ax command exists); LOGBOOK entry newest-first. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-ptxkqe ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is your predecessor leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision it, do not rebase it, do not commit on it, do not amend or revert the checkpoint, and never edit .task-board directly or self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint: exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Before you start, read the predecessor's landed delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree show --stat HEAD`) and its outcome resources so you extend it instead of restating it. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-kkh1an` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK stay purely additive; task-board.config.json must equal HEAD byte-for-byte). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured validation suite (30 commands as of trunk c3aae73 — STORY-260830-4qojoz added three bounded rpcwire fuzz gates; read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it) once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-kkh1an_results.md, TASK-260830-kkh1an_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-kkh1an_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-kkh1an --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n10|\n11|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n12|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n13|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n14|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n15|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n16|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n17|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n18|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n19|\n20|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n21|\n22|LANDED FACT YOU MUST NOT GET WRONG (found by the independent review of TASK-260830-1geqhj rev2, and it cost that leaf a whole round): `Observation.Winner.HasCheckpoint` / `Winner.Checkpoint` is a LEASE RECORD field, and lease records are immutable content-addressed blobs. `sessrepo.CreateLease` mints the epoch-1 lease with a NULL checkpoint, and only `CompareAndSwapLease` — a takeover, epoch >= 2 — mints a lease that carries one (Section 5.3: \"Null only for epoch-1 create; otherwise the validated materialized handoff base\"). No number of published checkpoints ever puts one on the epoch-1 lease. So any gate keyed on that field is VACUOUS for every session that never changed hands, which is the ordinary same-host story. The landed fact for \"the newest checkpoint\" is the chain fold: `sessstate.Reduce` -> `Projection.HasCheckpoint` / `Newest.ID`, derived from `checkpoint.created`, checkpointed `session.stopped` and `session.resumed` (Section 5.7 `newest_checkpoint_id`, Section 14.4). Key any window, admission or binding rule on that fold, and where a successor lease also carries a checkpoint, ASSERT the two agree rather than letting one silently win.\n23|\n24|INHERITED ADVISORIES from the independent review that accepted the predecessor leaf (RUN-260917-e644e0 accepted TASK-260830-1geqhj CR rev4 with no P1 and no P2, and recorded seven P3 items explicitly \"for the Story's follow-up leaves\" — that is you and TASK-260830-2056mm). None is a production defect on a path `Run` reaches today, but each is an unmeasured or under-specified seam in the code you are extending. Read them in TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-verdict-rev4.md and close the ones your scope touches, saying in your results which you closed and which you deliberately left to 2056mm:\n25|- - **P3-1 Unpinned member of the closure-source class: the journal-only restore path.** RV4-6 (`run.go`: read `observation.Winner.Checkpoint` instead of the fold's newest on exactly `Mode == ModeRestore && Winner.HasCheckpoint`, i.e. a restore with the journal required and NO required checkpoint) SURVIVED the whole committed suite twice. Witness `TestDX4_JournalOnlyRestoreClosureSource` (`logs/53-*`): the yolo→standard post-takeover world, restore with `MaterializationRequired` (journal from C1) and `CheckpointRequired=false` — pristine launches `standard`/`toStandard`, mapping empty; under the mutant it launches **`yolo` with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`** while `TestRunResto\n26|- - **P3-2 The pure core is not fail-closed on a missing closure.** `Decide` with `HasNewestCheckpoint=true`, no `CheckpointRequired` and empty `ProfileHeads` derives the SESSION HEAD: `TestR4HeadOnlyChangeAfterNewestNeverLaunches` (`logs/12`) — post-takeover world, C1 closure `standard`, a head-only `profile.changed → yolo` after the stop; `Run` launches `standard` in both modes (correct), `Decide` alone launches **`yolo` with the bypass mapping**. `Run` always supplies heads when the fold has a newest (an attested checkpoint carries 1..64 heads), so no `Run` path reaches this; the `Input` doc only says \"I/O-free callers supply them by hand\". Either refuse in `Decide` when the fold names a ne\n27|- - **P3-3 A remote-owned session whose newest checkpoint is absent from the LOCAL checkpoint store (or with no store bound) fails the run instead of reaching attach/takeover/park.** `TestR4RemoteOwnerNewestAbsentLocally` and `TestR4RemoteOwnerNoCkptStore` (`logs/12`): successor `(2,S)` on the remote host, chain folded locally, newest absent from the local `sessckpt` store → `Run` errors \"newest absent from the store\" / \"no checkpoint store\" with no decision, where `Decide` would return `attach_remote`. Fail-closed (no launch), but the after-restore step 4/5 outcomes become an error on a replica host with checkpoint lag; the closure is only needed on the launch path (local winner). Load the cl\n28|- - **P3-4 `EmitParked` does not bind the payload's `winning_lease_id` to the authoring lease.** `TestR4EmitParkedWinningLeaseMismatch` (`logs/13`): a `session.parked` event was appended under lease A with `payload.winning_lease_id = B`; RV4-13 (payload winner taken from `params.LeaseID`) SURVIVED ×2, so nothing distinguishes the decision's winner from the authoring lease. Through `Run` both come from `observation.Winner` (consistent); a direct caller can author a lying payload. Since `AuthorizeMutation` makes the authoring lease the winner, require `decision.WinningLeaseID == params.LeaseID` in `EmitParked` and pin it.\n29|- - **P3-5 Unpinned absence-vs-failure arm at `LoadCheckpoint`.** RV4-12 (any `sessckpt.Get` error laundered as absence) SURVIVED ×2. Witness `TestDX4_CorruptCheckpointBlobIsNotAbsence` (`logs/54-*`): a stored checkpoint blob corrupted in place (attestation failure, not ENOENT) — pristine: `Run` fails with the load error; under the mutant: \"newest absent from the store\". Both fail closed (no decision), the delta is the fact reported — the same class as rev3's P3-2. Add the corrupt-blob negative and a harness row.\n30|- - **P3-6 Reattach outcomes carry two terminal instance IDs.** `TestR4ReattachDescriptorNamesWhichInstance` (`logs/12`): on `reattach`, `Outcome.Binding.TerminalInstanceID` is the recorded child (1111) while `Decision.Descriptor.TerminalInstanceID` is the request's own/freshly minted instance (2222), built before the decision. `Decision.Descriptor` is documented as \"set on launch and reattach only\". Document that `Binding` is authoritative on reattach (or clear/rebuild the descriptor from the recorded child) so no caller targets a child that does not exist. Present since rev1; hygiene.\n31|- - **P3-7 Evidence archive hygiene.** `TASK-260830-1geqhj_rev4-evidence.tar.gz` is a tar of a shared scratch directory: its `README.md` is \"TASK-260830-24z2b3 producer evidence (rev4)\"; it carries `TASK-260830-24z2b3_results.md`/`_conformance-matrix.md`, a `TASK-260916-fsw7re` `logs-summary.txt`, `__pycache__/*.pyc` (3 files, listed in `MANIFEST.sha256`), `review-evidence-rev4.zip`, `mutants/run1|run2` (66 rows of a foreign task) and `mutants-run{1,2}.log`. The axpane evidence inside is genuine and matches my reruns (`mutants/pass1/` 54 raw logs with exits, `pass2-summary.log`, `axpane-test-v.log`, `ratio-check.log`, `cmd/cmd01..27`), but the archive breaks the task-scoped / no-`__pycache__` \n32|YOUR PREVIOUS CONSTRUCTION FAILED FOR A REASON THAT WAS NOT YOUR WORK — READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. Change Request revision 1 was built and its validation suite stopped at command 4 of 30 (`go test ./... -count=1 -v`) with exit 1, so the runtime recorded revision 1 as `changes_requested` even though NO reviewer ever saw it. The cause is a trunk defect that has since been fixed: `internal/resumesmoke/support_test.go` hard-coded a provider-call deadline of 2026-09-18T00:00:00.000Z, validated against a frozen 2026-09-17 fixture clock but enforced by `provhost` through `context.WithDeadline` on the REAL clock, so from 00:00Z the fake-provider child was killed before it answered and `go test ./...` went red on every tree. It was found by an independent reviewer on another task, reproduced on a clean trunk extract, and fixed as commit a12d1bd (BUG-260918-354b03, PR #53). Your Story branch base is e6fe5c5, which predates that fix, so the suite will fail the same way again until you move. Current origin/main is c3aae73 and carries BOTH the fix and STORY-260830-4qojoz (mesh RPC framing and negotiation, which also raised the configured suite from 27 to 30 commands). Run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-kkh1an` FIRST, then audit every path in `git diff --name-only e6fe5c5 c3aae73` that also appears in your `git status` — README.md, LOGBOOK.md and task-board.config.json all moved on trunk, so your edits must sit on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes, and the config must end byte-identical to the new base. Nothing about your scope changes: the Section 4.C lifecycle work and the seven inherited advisories stand exactly as written above. Everything you already wrote in the worktree is still there — read `git status --short` and `git diff` first, state in your results what you inherited, and verify it against this brief rather than redoing it.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b2ff-60d1-7b92-bcfc-8a4c766633f3"},"sequence":68,"recorded_at":1780531400000131,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71"},"call_id":"call_01a0b2ff89cc746d971859cb2ffdeeac","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-kkh1an/TASK-260830-kkh1an_rework-rev4.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-kkh1an, including TASK-260830-kkh1an_rework-rev3.md. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-kkh1an (implement-terminal-instance-state-machine, SECOND leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe). Change Request revision 3 was reviewed by RUN-260918-f6b44c (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED with NO P1. Read TASK-260830-kkh1an_review-verdict-rev3.md and TASK-260830-kkh1an_review-evidence-rev3.tar.gz first, and note how much is now CONFIRMED by the reviewer's own instruments: F1's same-key retry after `status` meets the behavioural bar at your seam AND at the reviewer's own engine seam with real SIGKILLs; F2's remote-winner arms fence and compose the landed gate; E1's create-row gates, E2's third-position rechecks and the session axis are pinned and their mutants die; the shipped harness reproduces 100/100 twice; the ratio re-derives to 84 of 84; the full configured suite is green; hygiene is clean. None of that is in scope — do not touch it.\n3|Two findings remain, and the reviewer marks BOTH as `repeat-of` the previous round's classes. That is the thing to take seriously: twice now the fix has closed the members that were witnessed in the verdict and left the rest of the same class open. Fix the class this time, and say in the results which members you enumerated and why the enumeration is complete.\n4|F3 (P2 production, `repeat-of: rev2 F2`) — `ObserveFencing` still never fences a stale incarnation whose observation carries NO live fencing grant: the lapsed-grant member and the no-grant / controller-restart member of the cold force takeover, under a local winner and a remote winner alike. A grant AUTHORIZES; it does not make an incarnation less stale, so the staleness decision must not be grant-gated. Land an exported staleness verdict in `internal/fencing` that decides the direction and tuple arms over a verified, well-formed winner WITHOUT the grant precondition, and compose it for `ObserveFencing`'s second question (or for both questions). Drive the no-grant and lapsed-grant members from `active`, `parked` and `quiescing`, under a local and a remote winner, keeping unverified / ambiguous / no-winner as no-transition. Ship a narrowing row per new arm plus the named regression test the `repeat-of` rule requires. NOTE this is a change to a LANDED package (internal/fencing) rather than to your own — the reviewer sanctions it explicitly, so take it, but keep it to the exported verdict and do not restructure anything else there. If instead the product decision is that fencing observation IS grant-gated, that is a legitimate answer — but then say so explicitly in `doc.go`, in TRACEABILITY and in the README sentence, and name the recovery route the final leaf owes for these members. Silence is the finding.\n5|E3 (P2 evidence, `repeat-of: rev2 E2`) — the per-effect authorization recheck is pinned on the lease-tuple axis only, so an authorization that EXPIRES between effects commits the remaining effects with the whole suite green under a one-line mutant. Commit `TestRV3W_M1_AuthExpiryRecheckedBeforeEachEffect` or its equivalent: a context whose deadline lies after the authorization expiry, `Now` advanced past the expiry after effect 1, asserting the literal `ax authorization expiry`, `after=unavailable`, `new_authorization` and exactly one committed effect. Add a harness row that KEEPS the tuple recheck and DROPS the expiry recheck. Consider a second fixture instant between the deadline and the expiry so the E1 `expired` member also kills on the receipt.\n6|P3-1..P3-4: commit the four witnesses (`TestRV3W_M2/M3/M4/M8_*` are committable as they stand) with their harness rows. P3-5: state the interim-proven member of the create recovery and its exit route.\n7|Procedure: your candidate sits on the predecessor checkpoint c61fc06 on a Story branch refreshed onto c3aae73. Do not refresh unless origin/main actually moves; if it does, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-kkh1an` and audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`, keeping README/LOGBOOK edits additive on the NEW content. You are a MIDDLE leaf: do NOT touch internal/traceability — the final leaf TASK-260830-2056mm carries the registry bindings and the pin. internal/fencing IS in scope for F3 as described above, and only for that. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. Re-run the full configured validation suite once (30 commands at trunk c3aae73 — read the count from the candidate config; command 5 is the race gate with `-timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs). Update TASK-260830-kkh1an_results.md with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path) AND a short paragraph for each of F3 and E3 naming the members you enumerated and why that enumeration is complete, update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive (a real gzip archive, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-kkh1an --role developer` so revision 4 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 2.5 hours in.\n8|PARTIAL-ATTEMPT RECOVERY: this task has lost runs to provider idle timeouts before. If a previous attempt died mid-way, the Story worktree still carries its partial edits — start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of assuming it is correct.\n9|Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 4.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b2ff-60d1-7b92-bcfc-8a4c766633f3"},"sequence":69,"recorded_at":1780531400000133,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71"},"call_id":"call_01a0b2ff8b2d769ab77cc8e6ab59e9b7","text":"<read-skill-result name=\"go-testing-tools\" status=\"ok\">\n<metadata>\npath: /Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nlocator: file:///Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nsource: user\nbytes_returned: 15216\nbytes_total: 15216\ntruncated: false\n</metadata>\n\n<skill-body id=\"go-testing-tools\">\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: &gt;\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    &quot;testing&quot;\n\n    &quot;github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit&quot;\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;), tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, &quot;third item&quot;)\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, &quot;ERROR&quot;)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 &gt; Level 2 &gt; Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: &quot;enter&quot;, &quot;tab&quot;, &quot;esc&quot;, &quot;space&quot;, &quot;backspace&quot;, &quot;up&quot;, &quot;down&quot;,\n// &quot;left&quot;, &quot;right&quot;, &quot;home&quot;, &quot;end&quot;, &quot;pgup&quot;, &quot;pgdown&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;, &quot;insert&quot;,\n// &quot;f1&quot;-&quot;f20&quot;, &quot;ctrl+c&quot;, &quot;ctrl+a&quot;...&quot;ctrl+z&quot;, &quot;alt+h&quot;, &quot;alt+enter&quot;,\n// single runes: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;r&quot;))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: &quot;cursor in bounds&quot;,\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor &lt; 0 || s.Cursor &gt;= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(&quot;cursor %d out of [0, %d)&quot;, s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &amp;TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;Execute&quot;, cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;Execute:&quot; + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n```\n\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n\n```go\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\n\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n```\n\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/&lt;name&gt;.golden` relative to the test file.\n\n```bash\n# Create or update golden files\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n\n# Update for a single package\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n```\n\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\n\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\n\n```\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n```\n\n### Step Details\n\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n- What state does it manage?\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n- What does View() render?\n\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n- Pure state logic -&gt; Level 1 (reducer tests)\n- Key bindings, view rendering -&gt; Level 2 (component tests)\n- Data loading, command execution -&gt; Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n- Visual appearance -&gt; Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n- Type assertion panic -&gt; `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n- View assertion failure -&gt; check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n- Snapshot mismatch -&gt; run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n- Mock assertion failure -&gt; check response key matches (`&quot;Execute:list&quot;` not `&quot;Execute&quot;`)\n\n---\n\n## Templates\n\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n\n| Template | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\n\n| Document | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n\n---\n\n## Setup\n\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n\nTo remove:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/deinit.sh\n```\n\n</skill-body>\n</read-skill-result>","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_skill","outcome":"success"}}}
