{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000dce3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b2ff-60d1-7b92-bcfc-8a4c766633f3"},"sequence":3276,"recorded_at":1780531400006547,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b5be48e9-caa3-42a3-a29a-786d769c2b71"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b321-528e-7052-bf43-a7c9570fa356"},"task_id":"01a0b321-528e-7052-bf43-a7c9570fa356","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b321-528e-7052-bf43-a7c9570fa356","chunk":"Read text file `internal/terminstance/fencing.go`.\n1|package terminstance\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/fencing\"\n5|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/terminalbackend\"\n6|)\n7|\n8|// ObserveFencing projects one AX fencing observation onto the Terminal\n9|// Instance lifecycle. It is the ONLY entry that produces stale_fenced:\n10|// no backend operation targets that state (the landed CheckTransition\n11|// proves it and the engine never synthesizes it), only AX fencing\n12|// observation does.\n13|//\n14|// The verdict is read entirely from the landed fencing projection: the\n15|// presented local token is authorized against the observation through\n16|// fencing.Authorize with the restore operation, and a ParkStaleOwner\n17|// park — the older-epoch arm and the same-epoch lease-mismatch arm\n18|// alike — moves the local observation to stale_fenced. The operation\n19|// must be launch-class (restore or activation): the input, mutation\n20|// and checkpoint entries refuse a stale token instead of parking it,\n21|// which would surface as an error and never fence. Restore is chosen\n22|// over activation because it gates terminal restore, this story's\n23|// domain; the two are equivalent on the stale arms.\n24|//\n25|// A stale token under a REMOTE winner fences too: the landed gate parks\n26|// remote_owner before it compares tuples (\"the operative fact beats the\n27|// credential\"), so the first verdict cannot distinguish a stale token\n28|// from the winning token under a foreign winner. The second question\n29|// asks the landed gate whether the presented token is stale RELATIVE TO\n30|// THE WINNER by authorizing from the winner's own host (LocalHostID set\n31|// to Winner.HolderHostID) and reading the park: stale_owner fences,\n32|// anything else — including the winning token authorizing cleanly —\n33|// leaves the state with the original remote park. Both questions run\n34|// inside the landed Authorize; no local tuple comparison exists here.\n35|//\n36|// A stale token refused by the gate's GRANT precondition still fences:\n37|// the grant arms (no grant, no clock reading, lapsed grant, unusable\n38|// policy) fire before the direction/tuple arms, so without a second\n39|// verdict a cold force-takeover incarnation — one that can never hold\n40|// a live grant, because VerifyFencingToken refuses its stale epoch\n41|// and grants never survive a controller restart — would never reach\n42|// stale_fenced. The landed StaleRelativeToWinner verdict decides the\n43|// direction/tuple arms over the verified well-formed winner without\n44|// the grant precondition, and a decided stale verdict fences from a\n45|// live incarnation exactly like the gate's own stale park. The\n46|// relative question needs no such fallback: it carries the direct\n47|// observation's grant facts unchanged and the grant arms do not read\n48|// LocalHostID, so a direct remote park implies the relative question\n49|// passes the grant arms.\n50|//\n51|// Only a live incarnation fences: creating, parked, active and\n52|// quiescing move to stale_fenced, while absent and stopped (no\n53|// incarnation exists for terminate-stale to target), stale_fenced\n54|// itself (already fenced: the transition is idempotent) and\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
